From a5fc255e223b01c8ce7ab5dae3ea9db80bad049f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MasherDon <62326358+MasherDon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:01:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 39/91] moe-stream : the host tier holds what a running prompt
 touched (SiegeLadder P9), on by default - a long prompt's ubatches walk
 layers 0..39 again and again and each touches about twice what the tier
 holds, so CLOCK, an LRU on a loop, evicted exactly what the next ubatch
 needed (a 23 454-token prompt at -ub 1536: 3.42% hits, every slot refilled
 ~29 times, 1.47 TB read from the drive in 164 s). While a prompt runs, a slab
 it fills or hits is held - never evicted for another - up to an even share of
 each pool per layer of that pool (so the decode after the prompt finds some
 of every layer), and what is past the share streams through a ring at the end
 of each pool (1/32 of it, FIFO, never held). The holding follows whole decode
 calls (llama_moe_stream::batch_begin from llama_context::decode): a call of
 at least LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_L2_SCAN_MIN tokens (64) begins it, anew from
 position 0, and more than two shorter calls in a row end it, so a lone short
 chunk inside a prompt does not and decode with several sequences does.
 SiegeLadder runs.md 4.62: the same prompt 159.0 / 159.9 -> 126.6 / 127.7 s
 (+25.5%), drive reads 1 473 -> 745 GB, tier hits 3.4 -> 51.2%, the 64 answer
 positions' logprobs identical to the bit in every pair; test4 prefill at -ub
 2048 2.2K +15.6%, 8.9K +20.3% (162-164 -> 195-196 tok/s), the decode right
 after +10%, answers identical. LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_L2_SCAN=0 = CLOCK throughout,
 as before. A review agent found no output, counter, lock or hand-arithmetic
 defect; its two speed findings (holding not ended by multi-token decode
 calls; the quota divided by every layer instead of the layers using the pool)
 are fixed here

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
